\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Data Shows a Strong Job Market, and That Worries the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street's weak start to 2024 carried into a third day, and stocks finished mixed on Thursday following reports showing the U.S. job market remains solid, though maybe a touch too strong.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 slipped 0.3 percent and is on track for its first losing week in the last 10. The Dow Jones industrial average eked out a gain of less than 0.1 percent and the Nasdaq composite fell 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens Boots Alliance\uc2  sank 5.1 percent after it nearly halved its dividend so it could hold onto more cash. That helped overshadow gains for airlines and cruise-ship operators, which recovered some of their losses from earlier in the week. Carnival steamed 3.1 percent higher, and \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  got a 2.4 percent lift.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks have broadly regressed this week after rallying into the end of last year toward record heights. Critics said the market was due for at least a breather following its big run, which fed on hopes inflation has cooled enough for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates sharply this year.\par \par \uc2 Rate cuts give a boost to prices for stocks and other investments, while also relaxing the pressure on the economy and financial system. Treasury yields in the bond market have already eased since autumn on expectations for such cuts, releasing pressure on the stock market.\par \par \uc2 But Treasury yields rose Thursday following reports showing the job market may be stronger than expected. The economy is in a delicate phase where investors want it to remain solid, but not too hot.\par \par \uc2 A healthy job market is of course good for workers and stamps out worries about an imminent recession. But too much strength could prod the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to keep interest rates high because it could keep upward pressure on inflation. And the Fed has already raised its main interest rate to the highest level since 2001.\par \par \uc2 One report from the government on Thursday showed fewer workers filed for unemployment benefits last week than expected. Another, from ADP Research Institute, said private employers accelerated their hiring last month by more than economists expected.\par \par \uc2 A more comprehensive report on the jobs market from the Labor Department will arrive on Friday. Economists expect that to show hiring slowed to 160,000 jobs last month from 199,000 in November.\par \par \uc2 ''If tomorrow's numbers show the same kind of strength and the economy keeps rolling along, it's fair to wonder why the Fed would be in a rush to cut rates,'' said \uc2 Chris Larkin\uc2 , managing director, trading and investing at E-Trade from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders are betting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates by twice as much this year as the central bank has indicated. Wall Street is also thinking the first cut could come as soon as March, and a stronger-than-expected economy makes such predictions less realistic. Critics had already called them overly aggressive.\par \par \uc2 A third report from S&P Global said that growth for financial businesses and others in services industries was a touch stronger last month than expected.\par \par \uc2 Following Thursday's data reports, the yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 3.99 percent from 3.91 percent late Wednesday. The yield on the two-year Treasury, which more closely tracks expectations for the Fed, climbed to 4.39 percent from 4.33 percent.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have already rallied in part on expectations for sharp cuts coming to interest rates soon. If the Fed doesn't cut as deeply and as quickly as expected, prices for stocks and other investments could be in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Peloton Interactive\uc2  jumped 13.9 percent after it announced a partnership to bring its workout content to TikTok.\par \par \uc2 APA fell 7.3 percent after it said it will buy \uc2 Callon Petroleum\uc2  in an all-stock deal valued at roughly $4.5 billion, including debt. \uc2 Callon Petroleum\uc2  gained 2.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, indexes were modestly higher in much of Europe and a bit lower in much of Asia.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240105ek150004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
