\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Labor Board Says \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Illegally Fired Musk Critics\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 931 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said the rocket company had wrongly dismissed eight people for a letter raising concerns about the chief executive.\par \par \uc2 Federal labor officials accused the rocket company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  on Wednesday of illegally firing eight employees for circulating a letter critical of the company's founder and chief executive, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to a complaint issued by a regional office of the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , the company fired the employees in 2022 for calling on \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  to distance itself from social media comments by Mr. Musk, including one in which he mocked sexual harassment accusations against him.\par \par \uc2 The letter circulated by the employees also called on \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , which has more than 13,000 employees, to clarify its harassment policies and enforce them consistently.\par \par \uc2 The labor board complaint said the company's president and chief operating officer, \uc2 Gwynne Shotwell\uc2 , had illegally restricted employees from circulating the letter, and it identified similar infractions by other executives and managers.\par \par \uc2 The case is scheduled to go before an administrative judge in early March unless \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  agrees to a settlement beforehand. A spokeswoman for the labor board said it was seeking make-whole remedies like reinstatement and back pay for the workers.\par \par \uc2 ''At \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  the rockets may be reusable, but the people who build them are treated as expendable,'' said Paige Holland-Thielen, one of the employees who were fired. ''I am hopeful these charges will hold \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and its leadership accountable for their long history of mistreating workers and stifling discourse.''\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk has sometimes taken a hard line toward his companies' employees, as when he laid off roughly half the work force at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now known as X, shortly after buying the company in 2022. He later fired roughly two dozen internal critics at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , which has lost about 80 percent of the 7,500 employees who worked there when the billionaire took over.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , where Mr. Musk is chief executive, has spent years litigating a case in which the labor board accused it of firing an employee for engaging in union activity. The board ruled in 2021 that the firing was illegal and ordered \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to reinstate the worker with back pay, a decision that a federal court affirmed. The company is appealing the case further.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department sued \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  in August, accusing it of discriminating against asylum-seekers and refugees in its hiring, but a judge has issued an injunction blocking that case from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 In December 2021, a former \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  employee published an essay detailing instances of harassment and groping by colleagues that she said went largely unaddressed after she reported them.\par \par \uc2 The essay provoked outrage within the company, which said it would begin an audit of its harassment policies.\par \par \uc2 The next spring, \uc2 Business Insider\uc2  reported that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  had paid $250,000 in 2018 to settle a claim in which an employee accused Mr. Musk of exposing himself and sexually propositioning her. Mr. Musk denied the accusation and joked about it on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not long after, a group of employees began brainstorming ideas for making the company less tolerant of harassment and drafting the letter. Ms. Shotwell was aware of the effort and appeared supportive of it, according to comments she left on an internal communications platform seen by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In mid-June 2022, several employees circulated their letter to colleagues. The letter called Mr. Musk's public comments ''a frequent source of distraction and embarrassment for us'' and urged the company to ''uphold clear repercussions for all unacceptable behavior, whether from the C.E.O. or an employee starting their first day.''\par \par \uc2 While some managers responded sympathetically, within a few hours Ms. Shotwell reprimanded two employees involved in writing and distributing the letter, Tom Moline and Ms. Holland-Thielen. ''Please stop flooding employee communication channels immediately,'' Ms. Shotwell said in an email, adding, ''I will consider your ignoring my email to be insubordination.''\par \par \uc2 The next day, the company fired Mr. Moline, Ms. Holland-Thielen and three other employees involved in organizing the letter. It fired four others connected to the letter in July and August 2022. (The labor board considered only eight firings because the ninth employee did not file a formal charge.)\par \par \uc2 The labor board complaint said that the firings had been retaliatory and that Ms. Shotwell and other \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  officials had interfered with the employees' rights to engage in concerted activities that are legally protected.\par \par \uc2 It also said a company vice president had broken the law by criticizing the letter at a meeting with employees a few days after it was circulated and ''inviting employees to quit if they disagreed with the behavior of chief executive officer \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .'' The Times previously reported that a company vice president had told workers that the letter was an extremist act and that Mr. Musk could do whatever he wanted at the company.\par \par \uc2 The complaint also said a senior human resources official had illegally created the impression of surveillance when she showed workers involved in drafting the letter screen shots of a chat they had conducted on a messaging app.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Mac\uc2  contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Mac\uc2  contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX fired Tom Moline, far left, and Paige Holland-Thielen after they circulated a letter critical of the company's founder, Elon Musk. Below, Mr. Moline's collection of employee mission patches. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JENNA SCHOENEFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240104ek1400041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
