\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 $12B\b0\par\par\uc2 By Marie Solis \par \uc2 422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pilots for \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  can expect their pay to soar in the new year. Just in time for the holiday travel rush, their union and the company reached a tentative deal worth $12 billion.\par \par \uc2 The contract fight grew contentious, and the airline and the union entered federal mediation last year to try to find a compromise. Though federal law prohibits pilots from walking off the job without exhausting the mediation process and taking other steps, the union's 11,000 members authorized a potential strike in May.\par \par \uc2 As negotiations wore on, Southwest also lagged farther and farther behind its rivals -- \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  -- which had finished bargaining with their pilots unions earlier in the year. But if they approve the agreement, pilots at Southwest will see a bigger payoff than their peers at those companies: The contract would boost wages 50 percent over the next several years, higher than the increases of 36 percent at Delta, 46 percent at American and 40 percent at United. (Pilot salaries at the major airlines are typically six figures.)\par \par \uc2 The union gains at these airlines are due in part to the advantage that pilots have in labor talks. When travel came booming back after the worst of the pandemic, their work was in high demand, and airlines had trouble keeping up. Southwest's rocky rebound stood out: On Monday, the airline was fined $140 million for last year's holiday meltdown, when Southwest canceled more than 17,000 flights, disrupting travel for some two million travelers.\par \par \uc2 ''Ken is trying to find his place in the world.''\par \par \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , an economist at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , explaining how the ''Barbie'' movie, one of the year's biggest trends, spoke to men's uncertain footing in the labor market. (Women gained 1.4 jobs for every one that men did in 2023.)\par \par \uc2 ''We want to throw holiday parties where the next Monday everyone shows up proud of who they were.''\par \par \uc2 Roy Bahat\uc2 , a venture capital investor with \uc2 Bloomberg Beta\uc2 , on the trend of holding office holiday parties in the afternoon rather than late into the night.\par \par \uc2 ''It's easy when you keep continually clicking and clicking and clicking, and then it's not.''\par \par \uc2 Liz Cisneros, a 23-year-old college student in Chicago who works part time at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , referring to the ''buy now, pay later'' programs online that can result in surprising debts.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page BU2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231224ejco0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
