\par\par \uc2 SQUARE FEET\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 It's Popular. It's Stylish. It's . . . the Cubicle?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ellen Rosen \par \uc2 1,393 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Among office designers and architects, cubicles are rarely mentioned. The once-ubiquitous fixture, so popular in the 1980s and '90s, has become vilified as a sign of the dehumanization of the work force. Design experts today say cubicles are a ''hard no.''\par \par \uc2 And yet cubicles, like scrunchies, are back, spurred by demand from employers and employees alike.\par \par \uc2 ''I frankly thought the cube market was dying,'' said Brian Silverberg, who sells refurbished and used office furniture with his brother, Mark, in their store, the Furniture X-Change in North Brunswick, N.J. ''We have sold more cubes in the last three years than in the five years before,'' he said, adding that 2024 would be ''bigger than this year.''\par \par \uc2 Covid-19 was an amplifier of a trend that preceded the pandemic. But as workers returned to the office after months of working at home, quiet spaces became more important, said Janet Pogue McLaurin of Gensler. ''We had seen a drop in effectiveness because of noise interruptions, disruptions and a general lack of privacy,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Global demand has pushed cubicles and partitions to a $6.3 billion market, which is expected to grow over the next five years to $8.3 billion, according to a 2022 report from Business Research Insights, a market analysis firm.\par \par \uc2 Furniture manufacturers had already recognized that workers wanted some privacy despite the tendency of employers to value collaborative areas more highly than individual work spaces.\par \par \uc2 Anyone who has ever worked in an office with benches ''hates the open plan,'' said Michael Held, vice president of global design at the furniture maker \uc2 Steelcase\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Working from home during the pandemic offered some relief from noisy co-workers, but it also brought new distractions, including constant interruptions by family members and roommates and the nagging temptation to do household chores. Employees cite a lack of focus as the biggest problem with remote work, said \uc2 Ryan Anderson\uc2 , vice president of global research and insights at \uc2 MillerKnoll\uc2 , the furniture maker, which tracks worker trends with the \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2  and the messaging platform \uc2 Slack\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a result, just as companies are trying to juggle remote work and in-office mandates, they are also deliberating the right mix of collaborative areas, conference rooms and individual spaces.\par \par \uc2 For example, at Grassi, a New York accounting and auditing firm with 500 employees, the offices have been reconfigured to hybrid spaces, emphasizing cubicles or semiprivate areas along with open collaborative spaces.\par \par \uc2 Some of the company's seven offices were ''too open with no dedicated private space,'' said Jeff Agranoff, the company's chief human resources officer. Now the firm has a combination of open and private spaces. (The company also eliminated reservation scheduling for desks, an arrangement known as hoteling. ''Everyone has a dedicated space,'' Mr. Agranoff said, ''because we were concerned that significant hoteling would deter people from coming back to the office.'')\par \par \uc2 Many employers now offer a variety of work spaces, including shared offices, conference rooms, phone booths and libraries, Ms. McLaurin of Gensler said. And, yes, cubicles.\par \par \uc2 Just don't expect to see six-foot-high panels -- those remain out of fashion. Instead, the new cubes offer what Mr. Held called ''sitting privacy'' with 54-inch-high panels.\par \par \uc2 And unlike the cubicles in films like ''Office Space,'' which satirized their commodified and sanitized look, the current iterations are ergonomic and flexible and may include lighting. They can be rectangular or rounded, with fixed or adjustable walls, and can accommodate multiple electronic devices.\par \par \uc2 Teams can adapt them to different needs, and some include sound-masking features. \uc2 Steelcase\uc2 , for example, has incorporated panels that absorb some sound waves, creating ''less echo in the space,'' Mr. Held said, while also reflecting out less noise.\par \par \uc2 MillerKnoll\uc2  has a workstation that ''is not so much a cube and not really a private office,'' but instead is a ''small enclosed environment that is comfortable physically,'' Mr. Anderson said.\par \par \uc2 Standing desks are often incorporated in both new or refurbished workstations. Some of Grassi's refurbished cubicles include glass walls. Arms can be attached to raise or lower monitors to accommodate different heights as well as video calls.\par \par \uc2 Demand for refurbished workstations has dropped from its pandemic spike, but it still eclipses prepandemic levels. With its increase has come a concomitant drop in office benches, said Trevor Langdon, the chief executive of Green Standards, a company in Toronto that resells, donates and recycles office furniture. More popular now are smaller setups that incorporate cubicles, he said, adding that his inventory ''suggests that our clients are holding on to their low-panel workstations.''\par \par \uc2 In a sense, cubicles have come full circle in terms of flexibility. In the 1950s and '60s, private offices surrounded open areas with secretaries clattering on typewriters -- think ''The Apartment'' or ''Mad Men.'' But Robert Propst, an inventor, came up with a novel idea: Create flexible, partly enclosed spaces to promote work.\par \par \uc2 He developed his original design -- ''the action office'' -- in the 1960s when he worked at Herman Miller, now a part of \uc2 MillerKnoll\uc2 . Mr. Propst, who disliked cubicles, was an early advocate of invoking urban planning concepts, like neighborhoods, into office layouts.\par \par \uc2 Because flexibility makes construction more expensive, cheaper, fixed versions became the norm, and isolated, off-putting partitioned work areas were the result. Before long, critics began deriding the tall, usually fabric-covered cubicles. Eventually, the six-foot-high walls gave way to lower dividers until the office again contained large open working spaces, replete with benches and couches.\par \par \uc2 The increased focus on collaboration in the 1990s and early 2000s led office designers away from cubicles, but there was a secondary impetus for the open-floor plan: cost. In high-rent cities like New York or London, ''putting everyone in a cubicle or office was too much, so the open-floor plan became very popular,'' Mr. Held said.\par \par \uc2 After long stretches of working from home during the pandemic, manufacturers are acknowledging the influence of residential design on office furniture. Some employees are taking this one step further by importing home d\u233\'00\'E9cor into their work spaces. Cubicle dwellers often post photos on sites like \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lucas Mundt, a logistics analyst at Simple Modern in Oklahoma City, had already helped co-workers hang photos, but he wanted to transform his cubicle into a faux wood cabin. After getting permission, he set to work over a weekend, when the office was empty. ''I wanted to do it big and over the top,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 He added laminate wood floors and covered the walls with a woodlike adhesive paper. He appended a picture of a window and, although he does not hunt, added two stuffed animals meant to replicate those often found in hunting lodges. The chandelier and the space heater -- which looks like a wood-burning stove -- are voice-activated.\par \par \uc2 The transformation was a hit in the office. The company's chief executive, Mike Beckham, was such a fan that he posted photos on social media and gave everyone in the office a $250 allowance -- about the amount Mr. Mundt estimated he spent -- to redecorate their cubicles.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mundt acknowledged that his renovation was beyond the norm. ''If I'm going to spend 40 to 50 hours a week there, I wanted it to feel comfortable and relaxing,'' he said. ''And I feel at home in the mountains.''\par \par \uc2 Jolena Podolsky in her cubicle, above and below, at Simon & Schuster in New York. With employees returning from remote work, quiet spaces are more important. Cubicle dwellers often post photos on sites like Pinterest and Instagram. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MAANSI SRIVASTAVA/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); Lucas Mundt, a logistics analyst at Simple Modern in Oklahoma City, turned his cubicle into a faux wood cabin, above and right, to feel relaxed. The company's chief executive loved it. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MICHAEL NOBLE JR. FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Joyce LeeAnn Joseph's decorated desk at Jazz at Lincoln Center in New York. (PHOTOGRAPH VIA JOYCE LEEANN JOSEPH); At the Schlitz Audubon Nature Center in Milwaukee, Zoe Finney's desk fits right in. (PHOTOGRAPH VIA ZOE FINNEY); Michael Kauffman's desk at the Clio Awards in New York includes a variety of figurines. (PHOTOGRAPH VIA MICHAEL KAUFFMAN) (B5) This article appeared in print on page B1, B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231223ejcn0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
