\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Hits a 2023 High on a Solid Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 712 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street climbed back to its best level in 20 months on Friday after a stronger-than-expected report on the U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose 0.4 percent, enough to clinch a sixth straight winning week for the index, which is its longest such streak in four years. Wall Street's main measure of health is now just 4 percent below its record set at the start of last year.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average rose 130 points, or 0.4 percent, and the Nasdaq composite gained 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Yields rose more sharply in the bond market following the report, which said U.S. employers added more jobs last month than economists expected. Workers' wages also rose more than expected, and the unemployment rate unexpectedly improved.\par \par \uc2 The strong data keep at bay worries about a possible recession, at least for a while longer, and stocks of some companies whose profits are closely tied to the strength of the economy rallied. Energy-related stocks had the biggest gain of the 11 sectors that make up the S&P 500, rising 1.1 percent as oil prices strengthened amid hopes for more demand for fuel.\par \par \uc2 Carrier Global\uc2  climbed 4.5 percent for one of the market's bigger gains after it said it agreed to sell its security business, Global Access Solutions, to \uc2 Honeywell\uc2  for $4.95 billion.\par \par \uc2 But the worry on Wall Street is that the remarkably resilient job market could also end up giving inflation more fuel. That could push the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to either raise its main interest rate further or at least keep it at its highest level since 2001 for longer than expected.\par \par \uc2 That in turn could dilute the central hope driving the stock market recently: Inflation has come down enough from its peak two summers ago for the Fed to finally halt its hikes to interest rates and begin cutting them next year.\par \par \uc2 ''The Fed is so afraid of 'giving up before the job is done' that it will err on the side of overdoing it,'' said \uc2 Brian Jacobsen\uc2 , chief economist at Annex Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the job data looked decent, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  economists said in a BofA Global Research report. They said the numbers back up their expectation ''that economic activity will slow, not crash.''\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 4.22 percent from 4.15 percent late Thursday. It had been on a general decline and relaxing the pressure on stocks since topping 5 percent in October, when it reached its highest level since 2007.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the two-year Treasury, which more closely follows expectations for the Fed, rose to 4.72 percent from 4.60 percent as traders pulled back on bets for how many times the Fed could cut rates in 2024.\par \par \uc2 High rates and yields hurt all kinds of investments, and they pack a particularly hard punch on stocks seen as the most expensive or requiring their investors to wait a long time for big growth.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , slipped 1.4 percent and was the heaviest weight on the S&P 500. A day earlier, it had leaped amid excitement about the launch of its latest artificial-intelligence offering. Other Big Tech stocks were stronger, with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  all rising.\par \par \uc2 Also on the losing end was RH. The home furnishings company slumped 14% after reporting weaker results for the latest quarter than analysts expected.\par \par \uc2 All told, the S&P 500 rose 18.78 points to 4,604.37. The Dow added 130.49 to 36,247.87, and the Nasdaq climbed 63.98 to 14,403.97.\par \par \uc2 Friday's jobs report is one of the last major pieces of data the Fed will get before it announces its next move on interest rates Wednesday. On Tuesday, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  will give the latest monthly update on how high inflation is for U.S. consumers.\par \par \uc2 The widespread expectation is still for the Fed to hold its main interest rate steady next week, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231209ejc90004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
