\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Rises Ahead of a Highly Anticipated Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 956 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street closed higher following its first three-day losing streak since Halloween.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose 0.8 percent Thursday and continued to hang near its highest level since March 2022. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.2 percent, and the Nasdaq composite added 1.4 percent. Big Tech pushed strongly upward on the market, led by a big gain for \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 . Treasury yields largely held steady after a report showed a slight uptick in the number of workers applying for unemployment. A potentially more impactful report on the job market will arrive Friday.\par \par \uc2 Big Tech stocks helped power the market higher, led by a 5.5 percent jump for \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 . They're Wall Street's most influential stocks because of their massive size, and they have been on huge tears so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Cerevel Therapeutics\uc2  also jumped 10.8 percent after \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  announced an $8.7 billion deal to buy the company and its pipeline of candidates for schizophrenia, Parkinson's and other diseases. \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  added 0.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Much of Wall Street's recent rally has been because of rising hopes that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is finally done with its barrage of hikes to interest rates, which are meant to get high inflation under control. That has anticipation high ahead of a report on Friday, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's latest monthly update on the job market.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wants to see the job market slow by just the right amount. Too much weakness would mean people out of work and a possible recession, but too much strength could add upward pressure on inflation.\par \par \uc2 So far, anticipation is rising that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can nail a perfect landing for the job market and overall economy. Inflation has been slowing since peaking two summers ago, and expectations are building that the Fed's next move will be to cut interest rates next year.\par \par \uc2 A report on Thursday said that slightly more U.S workers applied for unemployment benefits last week, though the number is not alarmingly high and hit economists' expectations exactly. That had both stock and bond markets relatively calm and waiting for Friday's report, which could be more impactful.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury edged up to 4.13 percent from 4.12 percent late Wednesday. It's been generally easing since topping 5 percent in October and hitting its highest level since 2007.\par \par \uc2 The drop in the 10-year yield over the last month, including after accounting for inflation, is one of the reasons that strategists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  say the S&P 500 looks like it's trading ''roughly in line with fair value,'' even after its nearly 9 percent rip through November. Expectations for a healthy economy have also helped to boost stocks.\par \par \uc2 But the path ahead could travel down one of several forks, depending in part on how quickly inflation continues to cool and whether the Fed does cut rates by as much as traders are expecting. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says traders ''are approaching the limits of what could plausibly'' be expected for rate cuts without a recession hitting in the near term.\par \par \uc2 ''We believe much of the optimistic scenario is already reflected in US equity prices today,'' the strategists led by Ryan Hammond wrote in a report.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  began its campaign early last year to drastically ramp up interest rates, traders have several times built up bets for an imminent halt to rate hikes and for potential cuts, only to be disappointed each time. While \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have hinted recently their main interest rate may indeed be at a peak, some have said it's too early to begin considering when cuts could come.\par \par \uc2 Hopes for easier rates help all kinds of investments, particularly those seen as the most expensive or promising big growth the furthest in the future. That's helped send Big Tech stocks to their huge gains this year.\par \par \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's jump on Thursday brought its gain for the year so far to just over 55 percent. A day earlier, it announced the launch of its \uc2 Gemini\uc2  artificial intelligence model. The announcement made few waves on Wall Street initially, and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's stock slipped Wednesday, but analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said in a report they ''are encouraged to see \uc2 Google\uc2 's progress on this major technology shift.''\par \par \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  was the single strongest force pushing the S&P 500 upward, but \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  all also rose at least 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Another winner was \uc2 JetBlue Airways\uc2 , which climbed 13.6 percent after it said it may report better results for the final three months of the year than it earlier expected. It also slightly lowered the top end of its forecast for fuel costs during the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices have been falling recently amid worries about demand from the global economy falling short of the available supplies. A barrel of benchmark U.S. crude slipped another 4 cents to settle at $69.34. It's down from $93 in late September.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the international standard, fell 25 cents to $74.05 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 On the losing end of Wall Street, \uc2 C3.ai\uc2  tumbled 11.4 percent after reporting weaker revenue for the latest quarter than analysts expected.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, the Nikkei 225 dropped 1.8 percent in Tokyo amid speculation about whether the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will ease off its ultra-easy policy on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Losses elsewhere in Asia and Europe were more modest.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231208ejc80004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
