\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.S. Job Openings Fell in October, Reverting to Prepandemic Levels\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News:\par \par \uc2 Job openings fell considerably in October, hitting the lowest level since March 2021, the Labor Department announced on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 There were 8.7 million job openings in October, down significantly from 9.3 million in September, according to the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey. That was lower than economists' expectations of 9.3 million openings.\par \par \uc2 The rate of layoffs was little changed, as was the rate of quitting, which generally reflects workers' confidence in their ability to find new employment.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: The state of the labor market affects interest rate policy.\par \par \uc2 The labor market is closely watched by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as it mulls its interest rate policy. A cooling labor market tends to fuel predictions that the Fed will not further increase rates, which have risen to a range of 5.25 to 5.5 percent from nearly zero in March 2022.\par \par \uc2 The labor market has been surprisingly resilient since the Fed started its rate increases in a campaign to tame inflation. But as the job market shows signs of cooling, so has consumer spending. Many companies told investors that in the most recent quarter customers were pulling back and spending less on products and more on services and experiences. The Fed's preferred inflation measure confirmed that consumer spending slowed in October.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investors are increasingly hopeful that the Fed is done raising rates. \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , recently suggested in a speech that the central bank would leave rates steady if data continued to point to a cooling economy. The 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield fell on Tuesday, reaching its lowest point since September, as investors expected interest rates to fall in the future.\par \par \uc2 A reduction in job opportunities discourages the Fed from raising rates or keeping them high too long because such a trend often foreshadows a recession. ''With this evidence coming in that the labor market is cooling substantially, I think it's raising the chances that the Fed is done with the rate hikes,'' said \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Background: Unemployment and openings have reverted to earlier levels.\par \par \uc2 Though the labor market is slowing, it remains a healthy landscape for workers. The unemployment rate ticked up in October, to nearly 4 percent, which is in line with prepandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 Job openings reached a record of more than 12 million in March 2022 and have trended down since. The last time job openings hovered around nine million -- where it is now -- was in the spring of 2021.\par \par \uc2 There are still ample opportunities for workers. The rate of hiring remained steady in October despite the decline in openings.\par \par \uc2 One difference is that layoffs are lower than they were before the pandemic. That probably reflects companies' decisions to reduce staffing by natural attrition rather than cuts.\par \par \uc2 ''This is perhaps the biggest sign that we still have a strong economy and labor market,'' said Sonu Varghese, a strategist at Carson Group, a financial advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Though inflation has slowed significantly since the Fed started raising rates in March 2022, it remains above the central bank's 2 percent target.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's preferred inflation measure fell to 3 percent in October from a year earlier. But without including food and fuel prices, which are volatile and less sensitive to the Fed's policy actions, the rate was 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 What's next: The November jobs report comes on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The November jobs report will be released on Friday by the Labor Department. Economists forecast that the unemployment rate will stay around 4 percent, with a gain of about 180,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 That report will be one of the last insights into the state of the labor market before the Fed's next policy meeting on Dec. 12 and 13.\par \par \uc2 Many companies reported that in the most recent quarter customers were pulling back and spending less on products and more on services. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TONY CENICOLA/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231206ejc60003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
