\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 After Averting Hotel Strike, Las Vegas Has More to Do\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 1,305 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two big unions reached contract agreements with the three largest resort operators ahead of a series of events crucial to the city's economic rebound.\par \par \uc2 Debra Jefferies, a cocktail waitress at the Horseshoe Las Vegas, spent much of the week wondering whether she would be walking a picket line, as she did in 1984 -- the last time there was a major strike among hospitality workers in the city.\par \par \uc2 ''There was solidarity back then, just like there has been right now,'' said Ms. Jefferies, 68. ''Each generation has stepped up to demand better working conditions.''\par \par \uc2 Nearly 35,000 union members, including Ms. Jefferies, had threatened to begin a strike on Friday against the city's three big casino operators after months of negotiations had failed to yield a new five-year labor agreement.\par \par \uc2 But last-minute maneuvering averted a walkout as the resort owners -- \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 , \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2  and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2  -- came to terms, one by one, on tentative contracts with the city's two most powerful unions.\par \par \uc2 The final agreement, with \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2 , came early on Friday, a few hours before the strike deadline. The deal, when ratified, would provide ''outstanding benefits and overall compensation to our employees,'' \uc2 Wynn\uc2  said in a statement. One of the two unions, Culinary Workers Union Local 226, said the contract featured the largest wage increase negotiated in its 88-year history.\par \par \uc2 A strike loomed as a major disruption to a series of big events, starting with the Las Vegas Grand Prix, a Formula 1 auto race along the Strip that is expected to draw hundreds of thousands of visitors late next week.\par \par \uc2 It was the latest crucible for Las Vegas and for Nevada, which has the highest unemployment rate in the nation -- currently 5.4 percent -- and has struggled to bounce back ever since the start of the pandemic shuttered the Strip for months.\par \par \uc2 The hotel occupancy rate remains lower than prepandemic levels. In September, it was around 82 percent, compared with 88 percent in 2019. And union officials say there are about 20 percent fewer hospitality workers in the city than before the pandemic. Even with lower occupancy rates, though, there have been some indicators of a boost: Fewer people are spending more money. Tax revenue is 35 percent higher than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Along with the Formula 1 race, Las Vegas is the site of the National Finals Rodeo in December and the Super Bowl in February.\par \par \uc2 Bill Hornbuckle\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 MGM\uc2 , said in a Wednesday earnings call that his company had sold more than 10,000 tickets to the Grand Prix and expected to bring in $60 million in extra hotel revenue in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 Those stakes made a labor agreement all the more crucial.\par \par \uc2 The dispute pitted Culinary Workers Union Local 226 and Bartenders Union Local 165 -- affiliates of the labor confederation \uc2 UNITE HERE\uc2  -- against Caesars, \uc2 MGM\uc2  and Wynn, which operate 18 hotels along the Strip and are the state's three biggest employers. Ted Pappageorge, the head of Local 226, likened the negotiations to landing ''three large planes at once.''\par \par \uc2 The unions pushed for contracts that would raise wages and ease concerns about the introduction of new technology that could affect jobs. Many hotels, for example, have reduced front desk staff and instead created mobile check-in counters in an effort to reduce waiting.\par \par \uc2 Another major factor the union focused on during the seven months of negotiations centered on daily room cleaning. Since the pandemic, many of the hotels along the Strip cut daily room cleaning services for guests -- a move, union leaders said, that cost them jobs. And lawmakers voted this year to end a state law, passed during the pandemic, that required hotel rooms to be sanitized daily. Strict rules that now mandate daily room cleaning were significant victories in contract talks.\par \par \uc2 ''Hospitality workers will now be able to provide for their families and thrive in Las Vegas,'' Mr. Pappageorge said. He added that the \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  contract would provide compensation increases ''far above'' those in the last contract, which amounted to a $4.57-an-hour increase overall in wages, health care and pensions.\par \par \uc2 Details of the tentative agreements have not been released, but the terms are expected to be similar across the three companies. Under the contract that expired Sept. 15, union members make $26 an hour on average.\par \par \uc2 Stephen M. Miller, an economics professor at the \uc2 University of Nevada, Las Vegas\uc2 , said the postpandemic sea change in the balance of power between management and labor was on clear display in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Miller said the government stimulus money during the pandemic gave laid-off workers, including many who worked in the culinary union in Las Vegas, the resources to reconsider their future employment path.\par \par \uc2 ''The labor market is involved in a large restructuring process, which has given labor more bargaining power,'' he said. ''The resurgence of strikes and threats of strikes is the observable outcome of that power shift.''\par \par \uc2 If a strike had occurred, Mr. Miller said, it would have been detrimental to the state's economy.\par \par \uc2 ''The economic recovery here in Nevada has been in fits and starts,'' he said. ''Neither side wanted a strike. It would have been terrible for the state's economy and reputation.''\par \par \uc2 Even before the labor ferment in the last year in the auto industry, Hollywood and other realms, Nevada's culinary workers were a particularly powerful force.\par \par \uc2 It was culinary union members -- who include housekeepers, cooks, doormen, laundry workers, bartenders and food servers -- whose political clout was vital in winning legislative approval of Covid-19 safety precautions.\par \par \uc2 And they often help sway elections as a powerful base for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, members knocked on more than 500,000 doors and helped \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  win the state by roughly two percentage points. Last year, during the 2022 midterms, they doubled their door-knocking efforts, helping Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  secure her re-election. (Despite their efforts, the incumbent Democratic governor, \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 , who faced fierce criticism over pandemic shutdowns, lost by a narrow margin.)\par \par \uc2 That kind of support may be crucial to Mr. Biden again next year in a swing state where a recent New York Times/Siena College poll showed him trailing his likely Republican opponent, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , by 10 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Friday, President Biden applauded the Culinary Union, saying, ''Las Vegas has a long union history and workers have been critical to the city's growth and success.''\par \par \uc2 ''All workers -- including hospitality workers -- should have good jobs with fair pay and benefits that give them the opportunity to provide for themselves and their families,'' said Mr. Biden, noting the past support he has received from members of the union.\par \par \uc2 Yusett Salomon was among the workers who knocked on doors for Democrats during the 2022 election. He has worked as a warehouse operator transporting pallets of food and plants at the Wynn for the past two years, earning $22 an hour.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Mr. Salomon sat inside a cavernous hotel conference room observing negotiations. ''There is no better time than now to fight for what we deserve,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Lynnette Curtis and J. Edward Moreno contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lynnette Curtis and J. Edward Moreno contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Yusett Salomon, above left, a worker at Wynn Resorts, said now is the time to fight. Ted Pappageorge is the head of Culinary Workers Union Local 226. Culinary workers are among the nearly 35,000 union members who voted in September to authorize a strike against Las Vegas casino operators. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MIKAYLA WHITMORE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; BRIDGET BENNETT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231111ejbb0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
