\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Vegas Unions and \uc2 MGM\uc2  Reach a Tentative Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 521 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal, the second in two days with a major resort operator, was announced on the day before a strike deadline set by two major unions.\par \par \uc2 Two unions representing hospitality workers announced on Thursday a tentative labor agreement with a second major Las Vegas hotel operator, \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2 , a day before a strike deadline set by the unions.\par \par \uc2 Culinary Workers Union Local 226 and Bartenders Union Local 165 said a deal had been reached on a five-year contract covering 25,400 workers at \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2 , which runs eight Las Vegas properties: the Aria, Bellagio, Excalibur, Luxor, Mandalay Bay, MGM Grand, New York-New York and Park \uc2 MGM\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The unions, which are affiliates of \uc2 UNITE HERE\uc2 , announced on Wednesday that they had struck a deal with \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 , another major resort operator in the city.\par \par \uc2 The unions said last week that their members would go on strike if an agreement with the city's three main resort operators was not reached by Friday. The unions are still negotiating with \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The unions have been negotiating with the resorts since April. The agreement would avert a strike at \uc2 MGM\uc2 's resorts, although the unions' members still need to ratify the new contract.\par \par \uc2 Ted Pappageorge, the head of Local 226, said in a statement that with the new deal, \uc2 MGM\uc2  workers ''will be able to provide for their families and thrive in Las Vegas.'' The unions said the agreement with \uc2 MGM\uc2  included the largest wage increases ''ever negotiated in Culinary Union's 88-year history,'' a workload reduction for some members and increased safety protections, among other benefits.\par \par \uc2 ''We're pleased to have reached a tentative agreement that averts a strike, gives our culinary union employees a well-earned boost to pay and benefits and reduces workloads,'' \uc2 Bill Hornbuckle\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 It's not yet clear how big of a pay increase union members will receive, but Mr. Hornbuckle, told analysts on an earnings call Wednesday that a deal would result in ''the largest pay increase in the history of our negotiations with the culinary union.'' He added that the company would harness ''technology and process improvements to help offset the incremental labor costs we expect.'' The deal with the union includes some protections from new technologies that would affect their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The deals with the resort operators were reached about a week before the Las Vegas Grand Prix, a Formula 1 race that winds through the Strip, where most of the resorts are. The event promises to be a major moneymaker for the city's hospitality industry: Mr. Hornbuckle said his company had sold more than 10,000 tickets to the event and expected to attract $60 million in extra hotel revenue that weekend.\par \par \uc2 MGM Resorts owns the Bellagio Hotel and Casino, above. MGM expects to attract $60 million in extra hotel revenue during next week's Formula 1 race. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOE BUGLEWICZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231110ejba0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
