\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Vegas Unions and Caesars Reach Tentative Deal as Strike Looms\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 708 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal was announced two days before a strike deadline set by two unions. The walkout threat still hangs over other resorts and the city's economy.\par \par \uc2 Unions representing hospitality workers in Las Vegas reached a tentative agreement on Wednesday with one of the city's three major resort operators, two days before a strike deadline that loomed just as tourists arrive for a major sporting event.\par \par \uc2 Culinary Workers Union Local 226 and Bartenders Union Local 165, which are affiliates of \uc2 UNITE HERE\uc2 , announced the tentative agreement on a five-year contract with \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 . Negotiations with \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2  and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2  are continuing.\par \par \uc2 Contracts for housekeepers, bartenders, cooks and food servers at the three companies and Caesars expired on Sept. 15, after being extended from a June deadline.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference on Wednesday afternoon, Ted Pappageorge, the head of Local 226, said the tentative agreement would provide compensation increases ''far above'' those in the last five-year contract, which amounted to $4.57 an hour overall in wages, health care and pensions. Members of the union make $26 an hour on average.\par \par \uc2 The culinary union said the deal with Caesars had been reached after 20 straight hours of negotiations and covered 10,000 workers. Those workers will have 10 days to ratify the contract. Caesars said in a statement that the accord would provide ''meaningful wage increases that align with our past performance, along with continued opportunities for growth tied to our future plans to bring more union jobs to the Las Vegas Strip.''\par \par \uc2 The Caesars properties in Las Vegas include Caesars Forum, Caesars Palace, Flamingo, Harrah's, Horseshoe, Paris, \uc2 Planet Hollywood\uc2 , the Cromwell and the Linq.\par \par \uc2 Both unions said last week that 35,000 members would walk off the job on Friday at 18 hotels along the Strip owned by Caesars, \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2  and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2 , posing a major threat to the city's economy. The last major strike of Las Vegas casinos occurred in 1984, when workers walked off the job for more than 60 days.\par \par \uc2 As Las Vegas prepared for the impact of a strike, crews began to block off roads and erect bleachers near the Strip, which will serve as the course for the Las Vegas Grand Prix, a big international auto race.\par \par \uc2 In early December, the National Finals Rodeo is planned for two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The local unions have been negotiating with the resorts since April over demands that include higher wages, more safety protections and stronger recall rights, a protection that prioritizes rehiring laid-off employees, such as those let go during the pandemic lockdowns or economic downturns.\par \par \uc2 In September, the union passed a strike authorization vote with 95 percent support. More recently, workers have picketed outside the major hotels, marching under palm trees in 80-degree heat with signs that read, ''One Job Should Be Enough,'' alluding to low pay.\par \par \uc2 During a series of rolling walkouts that began in July, thousands of housekeepers, front desk clerks and other hospitality workers from several Southern California hotels have been on strike at various times; in Michigan, employees at \uc2 MGM Grand Detroit\uc2  have been on strike since mid-October.\par \par \uc2 For years, the culinary union, which represents 60,000 hospitality workers in Nevada, has been a powerful political force, one seen as a critical base for Democratic candidates in the state and nationally. In 2020, the ground operation and door-knocking campaign by union members helped propel \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  to a narrow victory in the state.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of his re-election campaign next year, President Biden trailed former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  by 10 percentage points in the state in a recent New York Times/Siena College poll.\par \par \uc2 During a trip last month, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited the culinary union's headquarters and praised the workers as the ''true champions for working people.''\par \par \uc2 Lynnette Curtis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Lynnette Curtis contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 At a meeting in September, union workers cheered as members voted to authorize a strike against the three biggest resort operators in Las Vegas. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BRIDGET BENNETT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231109ejb90004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
