\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Actors' Union Receives 'Best and Final' Offer From Studios\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Sperling \par \uc2 412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , which has been on strike for 114 days, did not give a timeline for when it would respond.\par \par \uc2 The negotiating committee of the actors' union, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , told its members on Saturday that it had received a ''Last, Best and Final Offer'' from the major entertainment studios as a strike that has brought much of Hollywood to a standstill continued for a 114th day.\par \par \uc2 ''We are reviewing it and considering our response within the context of the critical issues addressed in our proposals,'' the negotiating committee said. They did not say when they would respond to the offer, which came after an hourlong video conference call that included top studio executives.\par \par \uc2 Included in the offer was a wage increase that could be the highest in four decades, according to a person familiar with the offer who spoke on condition of anonymity because of the sensitivity of the negotiations. The studios also offered the actors a new way to determine residuals for streaming programs based on performance metrics, and protections on artificial intelligence, including consent and compensation requirements. The studios also offered an increase to the pension and health funds.\par \par \uc2 The virtual meeting on Saturday featured the largest group of top entertainment executives yet to engage with the negotiating committee, underscoring the urgency with which the studios want to return to work in an effort to salvage the fall television season and ensure next summer's theatrical box office will not be disrupted.\par \par \uc2 Four executives have spearheaded the talks: \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 's Studio Group chairwoman and chief content officer, \uc2 Donna Langley\uc2 ; the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  co-chief executive \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 ; the \uc2 Disney\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Robert A. Iger\uc2 ; and \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 . They were joined by the \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  chief executive Brian Robbins; the co-chairs of entertainment for \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Dana Walden\uc2  and \uc2 Alan Bergman\uc2 ; \uc2 Mike Hopkins\uc2  and Jennifer Salke from Amazon Studios; the \uc2 Sony Pictures\uc2  chairman \uc2 Tony Vinciquerra\uc2 ; and Jamie Erlicht and Zack Van Amburg of Apple Studios.\par \par \uc2 The entertainment business has been at a standstill for months because of strikes by writers, who walked out in May, and actors, who joined them in July. The writers' strike was resolved a little over a month ago, but the actors' work stoppage has continued to grind on, leaving thousands out of work for close to six months.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A25. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231105ejb50004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
