\par\par \uc2 Express; SECTB\par \b \uc2 What's Behind the Pharmacy Workers' Walkouts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Levenson \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The actions coincided with a period of increasing labor activism by workers in other sectors, including the auto industry and Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Some pharmacy workers are calling in sick or walking off the job this week to protest what they call inadequate staffing and increasing work requirements, according to protest organizers, and say it's harder to do their jobs safely.\par \par \uc2 The protests by nonunion workers, called Pharmageddon on social media, are affecting some CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  locations, according to organizers and workers. They are happening during a period of increasing labor activism by workers in other sectors, including the auto industry and Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 Bled Tanoe, a pharmacist in Oklahoma City who used to work for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and now works for a hospital, said she was helping to spread the word about the walkouts because she was concerned that pharmacy chains had been telling workers for years to ''work faster and work with less help.''\par \par \uc2 ''Pharmacies are not OK,'' Ms. Tanoe said. ''Your local \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS and \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  is not OK. It's a soup of danger, with ingredients from companies who have lost the core belief of what we do, which is patient care and patient focus.''\par \par \uc2 How many workers are walking out?\par \par \uc2 Without a union to coordinate the collective action, it is ''hard to say'' how widespread the protests are, said Shane Jerominski, a former \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  pharmacist in Southern California who has been coordinating the actions on his \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page, The Accidental Pharmacist.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the first of three planned days of walkouts, Mr. Jerominski said that ''thousands of stores were understaffed'' and 25 pharmacies closed in 15 states, including New York, Pennsylvania, Connecticut, Florida, Texas and Illinois.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  said in a statement on Tuesday that, since Monday, it had seen ''only three pharmacies out of nearly 9,000 close to due to work force disruption and no more than a handful of pharmacists walk out.''\par \par \uc2 CVS said in a statement on Tuesday that it was ''serving patients across our footprint today, and we're not seeing any unusual activity regarding unplanned pharmacy closures or pharmacist walkouts.''\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  said that it was ''unaware of plans by our pharmacists to participate in this activity.''\par \par \uc2 The protest is being organized on social media.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jerominski said that workers had organized on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and Reddit, rallying around demands for more staffing as well as better pay and guaranteed hours for pharmacy technicians.\par \par \uc2 Many were inspired, he said, by about two dozen CVS pharmacists who walked off the job in the Kansas City, Mo., area in September.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Pharmacists Association\uc2  said in a statement supporting the walkouts that pharmacists had contended for years with ''workplace issues, leading to frustrations and burnout, affecting your mental health and well-being.''\par \par \uc2 The association blamed, among other factors, quotas on the number of prescriptions filled per hour and vaccines administered per day.\par \par \uc2 What do workers say?\par \par \uc2 Nathan Fuller, 34, a pharmacist at a \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  in Thornton, Colo., said he called in sick on Monday and Tuesday to protest a lack of adequate staffing, which he said was jeopardizing patient safety.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  increased the scheduling of vaccines last year, allowing patients to double- and triple-book the same time slot, Mr. Fuller said. The technicians who administer vaccines could not keep up, and several said, '''Enough is enough' and just walked out of the pharmacy,'' Mr. Fuller said.\par \par \uc2 ''People were crying, people were broken down,'' Mr. Fuller said. He said he met with \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  leaders to emphasize the need for more resources or flexibility in scheduling vaccines, but was told, '''This is happening, whether you staff it or not.'''\par \par \uc2 He said he was concerned that when employees were stretched thin, ''the public is the one often suffering.''\par \par \uc2 ''We have people with burnout, dealing with exhaustion and emotional distress and mental health issues, and that can lead to unsafe environments for people doing jobs that require a lot of attention to detail,'' Mr. Fuller said.\par \par \uc2 Christine Settie, 64, said she quit her job last week as a pharmacy technician at a CVS in Waynesburg, Pa., because ''I just feel like they did not appreciate their employees.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Settie said she was earning $18.19 an hour, which she called ''pitiful,'' after 39 years on the job.\par \par \uc2 She handled the drive-through window, the front counter and the phones, and also counted pills. She was often the only worker other than the pharmacist on duty, she said.\par \par \uc2 CVS, she said, cut her hours to 30 a week from 40, forcing her to get a second job as a private nurse for a family to help her pay the bills.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Kolins Givan, an associate professor of labor studies at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 , said that such concerns were not limited to pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a symptom of what we see in lots of areas of health care, which is that various kinds of health care providers are motivated by profit and revenue, and if they can squeeze staff costs, they do,'' Professor Givan said.\par \par \uc2 ''You see this in nursing, as well,'' she said. ''Staffing shortages really get into a vicious cycle where the more short-staffed you are, the harder it is to do the job properly, and that makes more staff frustrated and demoralized and likely to leave.''\par \par \uc2 What do the pharmacies say?\par \par \uc2 CVS said it was ''engaging in a continuous two-way dialogue with our pharmacists to directly address any concerns they have.''\par \par \uc2 The company added, ''In response to recent feedback from our pharmacy teams, we're making targeted investments to address their key concerns, including enabling teams to schedule additional support as needed, enhancing pharmacist and technician recruitment and hiring, and strengthening pharmacy technician training.''\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  said: ''We have taken steps over the last two years to improve pharmacists' experience, advance the profession and enable them to provide the high-value care they were trained to do.''\par \par \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  said, ''We believe that our efforts in recent years and months to improve work/life balance and working conditions for our pharmacists is evidence of our commitment to the team.''\par \par \uc2 Facing a series of widespread job actions by its pharmacy workers, CVS said it was ''engaging'' with pharmacists ''to directly address any concerns they have.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY PHILIP CHEUNG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231102ejb20004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
