\par\par \uc2 PAUL KRUGMAN\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Autoworkers Strike a Blow for Equality\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Krugman \par \uc2 917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's not officially over yet, but the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  appear to have won a significant victory. The union, which began rolling strikes on Sept. 15, now has tentative agreements with Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  (which I still think of as Chrysler) and, finally, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All three agreements involve a roughly 25 percent wage increase over the next four and a half years, plus other significant concessions. Autoworkers are a much smaller share of the work force than they were in Detroit's heyday, but they're still a significant part of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, this apparent union victory follows on significant organized-labor wins in other industries in recent months, notably a big settlement with \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , where the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  represent more than 300,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 And maybe, just maybe, union victories in 2023 will prove to be a milestone on the way back to a less unequal nation.\par \par \uc2 Some history you should know: Baby boomers like me grew up in a nation that was far less polarized economically than the one we live in today. We weren't as much of a middle-class society as we liked to imagine, but in the 1960s we were a country in which many blue-collar workers had incomes they considered middle class, while extremes of wealth were far less than they have since become. For example, chief executives of major corporations were paid ''only'' 15 times as much as their average workers, compared with more than 200 times as much as their average workers now.\par \par \uc2 Most people, I suspect, believed -- if they thought about it at all -- that a relatively middle-class society had evolved gradually from the excesses of the Gilded Age, and that it was the natural end state of a mature market economy.\par \par \uc2 However, a revelatory 1991 paper by \uc2 Claudia Goldin\uc2  (who just won a richly deserved Nobel) and Robert Margo showed that a relatively equal America emerged not gradually but suddenly, with an abrupt narrowing of income differentials in the 1940s -- what the authors called the Great Compression. The initial compression no doubt had a lot to do with wartime economic controls. But income gaps remained narrow for decades after these controls were lifted; overall income inequality didn't really take off again until around 1980.\par \par \uc2 Why did a fairly flat income distribution persist? No doubt there were multiple reasons, but surely one important factor was that the combination of war and a favorable political environment led to a huge surge in unionization. Unions are a force for greater wage equality; they also help enforce the ''outrage constraint'' that used to limit executive compensation.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, the decline of unions, which now represent less than 7 percent of private-sector workers, must have played a role in the coming of the Second Gilded Age we live in now.\par \par \uc2 The great decline of unions wasn't a necessary consequence of globalization and technological progress. Unions remain strong in some nations; in Scandinavia, the great majority of workers are still union members. What happened in America was that workers' bargaining power was held back by the combination of a persistently slack labor market, with sluggish recoveries from recessions and an unfavorable political environment -- let's not forget that early in his presidency, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  crushed the air traffic controllers' union, and his administration was consistently hostile to union organizing.\par \par \uc2 But this time is different. Research by \uc2 David Autor\uc2 , \uc2 Arindrajit Dube\uc2  and Annie McGrew shows that a rapid recovery that has brought unemployment near to a 50-year low seems to have empowered lower-wage workers, producing an ''unexpected compression'' in wage gaps that has eliminated around a quarter of the rise in inequality over the previous four decades. The strong job market has probably encouraged unions to stake out more aggressive bargaining positions, a stance that so far seems to be working.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I constantly encounter people who believe that the recent economic recovery has disproportionately benefited the affluent. The truth is exactly the opposite.\par \par \uc2 The political ground also seems to be shifting. Public approval of unions is at its highest point since 1965, and Joe Biden, in a presidential first, joined an autoworker picket line in Michigan in September to show support.\par \par \uc2 None of what's happening now seems remotely big enough to produce a second Great Compression. It might, however, be enough to produce a Lesser Compression -- a partial reversal of the great rise in inequality since 1980.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this doesn't have to happen. A recession could undermine workers' bargaining power. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who also visited Michigan but spoke at a nonunion shop, returns to the White House, you can be sure that his policies will be anti-union and anti-worker. And Mike Johnson, the new speaker of the House, has an almost perfect record of opposing policies supported by unions.\par \par \uc2 So the future is, as always, uncertain. But we might, just might, be seeing America finally turn back toward the kind of widely shared prosperity we used to take for granted.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231031ejav00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
