\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Hollywood Studios Are Growing Optimistic About Reaching a Deal to End the Actors' Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brooks Barnes \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The entertainment companies are growing optimistic that the work stoppage may end soon, though some issues remain unresolved, people briefed on the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Following several productive days at the negotiating table, Hollywood studios are growing optimistic that they are getting closer to a deal to end the 108-day actors' strike, according to three people briefed on the matter.\par \par \uc2 These people, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because of the sensitivity of the labor situation, cautioned on Sunday that some issues remain unresolved with the actors, including protections around the use of artificial intelligence technology to create digital replicas of their likenesses without payment or approval. But other knots had started to become untangled, the people said.\par \par \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , as the actors' union is known, had been asking for an 11 percent raise for minimum pay in the first year of a contract, for instance. Studios had insisted that they could offer no more than 5 percent, the same as had recently been given (and agreed to) by unions for writers and directors. Early last week, however, studios lifted their offer to 7 percent. By Friday, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  had eased its demand to 9 percent.\par \par \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  did not respond to requests for comment. The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which negotiates on behalf of the major entertainment companies, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 In an email to \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members on Friday night, the union's negotiating committee said, ''We completed a full and productive day.'' On Saturday, the union sent a routine reminder about pickets planned the coming week, including one scheduled for Wednesday at \uc2 Walt Disney Studios\uc2 . The sides continued to negotiate on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Last week, studio executives made it known -- in conversations with filmmakers, agents, reporters and actors themselves -- that a deal must be done (or nearly so) by the end of this week, or else sets were likely to remain dark for another two months.\par \par \uc2 Put another way, unless talks speed up, January could be the soonest that casts (and crews) see paychecks.\par \par \uc2 Brinkmanship? Of course. It's a standard part of any strike. The companies, however, said they were simply pointing to the calendar. It will take time to reassemble creative teams, a process complicated by the coming holidays. Preproduction (before anyone gathers on a set) for new shows can take up to 12 weeks, with movies taking roughly 16 weeks. Bake in the time for contract ratification by the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members.\par \par \uc2 More than 4,000 mostly workaday actors responded on Thursday with an open letter to their union, saying, ''We have not come all this way to cave now.'' They added, ''We cannot and will not accept a contract that fails to address the vital and existential problems that we all need fixed.''\par \par \uc2 At the same time, some stars have pressured union leaders to approach negotiations with greater urgency. Out-of-work crew members have also grown increasingly frustrated with the Hollywood shutdown. The International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, which represents 170,000 crew members in North America, has estimated that its West Coast members alone have lost more than $1.4 billion in wages.\par \par \uc2 For their part, companies are under pressure to salvage their spring television schedules and movie lineups. On Friday, \uc2 Disney\uc2  delayed a live-action version of ''Snow White,'' which had been scheduled for March 26, because it would be impossible to finish in time. Earlier in the week, Paramount pushed back Tom Cruise's next ''Mission: Impossible'' movie, along with ''A Quiet Place: Day One,'' starring Lupita Nyong'o.\par \par \uc2 The entertainment business has been at a standstill for months because of strikes by writers, who walked out in May, and actors, who joined them in July. The writers' strike was resolved last month, prompting hopes of a speedy resolution between studios and the actors' union. Instead, the process has been slow.\par \par \uc2 Talks between the sides restarted on Tuesday after breaking down earlier in the month over a union proposal for a per-subscriber fee from streaming services, which \uc2 Netflix\uc2 's co-chief executive \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  publicly dismissed as a ''levy'' and ''a bridge too far.'' \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  accused studio executives of ''bully tactics.''\par \par \uc2 It is unclear how the streaming issue might be resolved. But there is real hope in Hollywood that people may soon be back to work.\par \par \uc2 ''At this time, we have no concrete information from any studio,'' Michael Akins, an International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees official in Georgia, wrote to members on Friday. ''But the writing is clearly on the wall that the industry shutdown is in its final days.''\par \par \uc2 John Koblin and Nicole Sperling contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 John Koblin and Nicole Sperling contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B7. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231030ejau00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
