\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 U.A.W. Reaches a Tentative Deal With \uc2 Stellantis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  announced the deal with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent of Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram. It also expanded its strike against G.M.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  announced on Saturday that it had reached a tentative agreement on a new labor contract with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company of Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram.\par \par \uc2 The agreement came three days after the union and \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  announced a tentative agreement on a new contract. The two deals contain many of the same or similar terms, including a 25 percent general wage increase for U.A.W. members as well as the possibility for cost-of-living wage adjustments if inflation flares.\par \par \uc2 ''We have won a record-breaking contract,'' the U.A.W. president, Shawn Fain, said in a video posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . ''We truly believe we got every penny possible out of the company.''\par \par \uc2 Shortly after announcing the tentative agreement with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the union expanded its strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , calling on workers to walk off the job at the company's plant in Spring Hill, Tenn. The plant makes sport utility vehicles for G.M.'s Cadillac and GMC divisions.\par \par \uc2 Under the tentative new contract with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , Mr. Fain said, the company has agreed to reopen a plant in Belvidere, Ill., to produce a midsize pickup truck and to rehire enough workers to staff two shifts of production.\par \par \uc2 The union also won commitments to keep an engine plant in Trenton Mich., open, and to keep and expand a machining plant in Toledo, Ohio. According to the union, these moves will create up to 5,000 new U.A.W. jobs.\par \par \uc2 The union also won the right to strike if the company closes any plant and if it fails to follow through on its promised investment plans, Mr. Fain said.\par \par \uc2 ''If the company goes back on their words on any plant, we can strike the hell out of them,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fain said \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  workers would now return to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said, ''We look forward to welcoming our 43,000 employees back to work and resuming operations to serve our customers.''\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  will require approval by a union council that oversees negotiations with the company, and then ratification by U.A.W. members. The council will meet on Thursday, Mr. Fain said.\par \par \uc2 The deal with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  means that only \uc2 General Motors\uc2  has not yet reached an agreement with the U.A.W.\par \par \uc2 Erik Gordon\uc2 , a business professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  who follows the auto industry, said the new contracts impose higher labor costs on the Detroit manufacturers as they are ramping up production of electric vehicles and are competing with rivals who operate nonunion plants.\par \par \uc2 ''The Detroit Three enter a new, dangerous era,'' he said. ''They have to figure out how to transition to EVs and do it with a cost structure that puts them at a disadvantage with global competitors.''\par \par \uc2 The union's contracts with the three automakers expired on Sept. 15. Since then, the union has called on more than 45,000 autoworkers at the three companies to walk off the job at factories and at 38 spare-parts warehouses across the country.\par \par \uc2 The most recent escalation of the strike at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  came on Monday when the U.A.W. told workers to go on strike at a Ram plant in Sterling Heights, Mich., that makes the popular 1500 pickup truck. The strike has halted the production of Jeep Wranglers and Jeep Gladiators at a plant in Toledo, Ohio, and 20 \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  parts warehouses.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the union has negotiated similar contracts with all three automakers, a method known as pattern bargaining. Like the contract it hammered out with Ford, the tentative \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  deal would lift the top U.A.W. wage from $32 an hour to more than $40 over four and a half years. That would allow employees working 40 hours a week to earn about $84,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , G.M. and Ford began negotiating with the U.A.W. in July. The companies have sought to limit increases in labor costs because they already have higher labor costs than automakers like \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and \uc2 Honda\uc2  that operate nonunion plants in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The three large U.S. automakers are also trying to control costs while investing tens of billions of dollars to develop new electric vehicles, build battery plants and retool factories.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which is based in Amsterdam, was created in 2021 by the merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  and \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , the French automaker. The company's North American business, based near Detroit, is its most profitable.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  surprised analysts recently by posting much stronger profits than G.M., which is the largest U.S. automaker by sales. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  earned 11 billion euros ($11.6 billion) in the first half of the year while G.M. made nearly $5 billion.\par \par \uc2 Noam Scheiber contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Noam Scheiber contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Pickets at a Stellantis site. ''We have won a record-breaking contract,'' the U.A.W. president, Shawn Fain, said after a deal was made. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DANIEL LOZADA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A23. \par 