\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Not in U.A.W. But Still Part Of the Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 1,662 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The three U.S. automakers say they are already at a disadvantage to nonunion rivals while labor leaders hope that big gains in negotiations will inspire workers in Southern states to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of people who work for \uc2 Toyota\uc2  in Kentucky, \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  in Alabama or \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in Texas are technically not involved in the high-stakes negotiations taking place between labor and management in and around Detroit.\par \par \uc2 But they are very much a presence.\par \par \uc2 Executives at \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent of Chrysler, invoke nonunion automakers, many of them in the South, as a competitive threat that makes it impossible for them to meet striking workers' demands for big raises, more generous benefits and better working conditions.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and others are loving this strike because they know the longer it goes on, the better it is for them,'' \uc2 Bill Ford\uc2 , the executive chair of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , said in Michigan last week. ''They will win, and all of us will lose.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  sees such statements as an attempt to play workers off one another. It views the strikes, entering their sixth week, as a first step toward better pay for not only U.A.W. members but also the nonunion workers that it plans to recruit in the future.\par \par \uc2 ''We won't be used in this phony competition,'' Shawn Fain, the U.A.W. president, said on Friday, reacting to Mr. Ford's speech. He added, ''Nonunion autoworkers are not the enemy. Those are our future union family.''\par \par \uc2 The pay gap between union and nonunion factories has long been a point of contention. Some industry executives have argued that high union wages were a big reason G.M. and Chrysler had to resort to bankruptcy after the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and progressive lawmakers have asserted that the growth of nonunion manufacturing, mostly in the South but also in the Midwest and West, has helped to erode the middle class over the past several decades.\par \par \uc2 Veteran union autoworkers tend to make more than production workers who are not represented by unions. They often have more say in their schedules and overtime work.\par \par \uc2 But starting pay at Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  factories can be lower than at nonunion factories. And the pay of nonunion workers at Southern auto plants tends to go further because the cost of living there is lower than it is in the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Even the geographic divide between union and nonunion plant is not always as clear as it may seem. \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and \uc2 Honda\uc2  have plants in the South, where unions are weak, but they also have factories in Ohio and Indiana, where unions are stronger. And G.M. and Ford have union operations in Kentucky, Tennessee and Texas.\par \par \uc2 The debate about auto industry wages has become more urgent as automakers invest billions to build factories that produce batteries for electric cars. Most of those factories are being built in Southern states, like Georgia and Tennessee, where local laws make it more difficult for unions to organize a factory.\par \par \uc2 ''A good agreement with the Detroit Three would be powerful because it gives union organizers better arguments for joining the union,'' said Ian Greer, a Cornell research professor who studies the effect of electric vehicles on labor.\par \par \uc2 Even with a union wage of almost $32 an hour from her job assembling chassis at a Ford factory in Chicago, Schataan Lyke said she did not have it easy. She is the sole breadwinner for three children and worries about how to afford a prom dress for her oldest.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lyke, who has been on strike, said she was glad to have the union behind her. ''You've got someone on the outside fighting for you,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Lyke, 37, has it better than people doing similar work in the South. At a \uc2 Nissan\uc2  factory in Canton, Miss., Morris Mock, 49, makes about $1 less than Ms. Lyke per hour even with more than 20 years of experience, he said.\par \par \uc2 An attempt to unionize \uc2 Nissan\uc2  in 2017 failed to win enough support from workers. That means Mr. Mock, one of the people who led the union drive, will not benefit directly from the contract that the U.A.W. works out with automakers. But he said he was glad the union was fighting to protect wages as the industry switched to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 ''The market is about to change,'' Mr. Mock said. ''I'm glad that they understand that we must put workers first.''\par \par \uc2 Government statistics suggest wide regional pay gaps. Michigan autoworkers make 22 percent more than production workers in Tennessee, 23 percent more than South Carolina workers and 28 percent more than Alabama workers, according to a Census Bureau survey. Those figures include people who work for suppliers, where pay is often lower than in factories that assemble vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Some labor experts said the bigger difference between union and nonunion autoworkers had less to do with pay and more with things like mandatory overtime and the scheduling of shifts. Union workers tend to have more say in those matters.\par \par \uc2 The auto industry has been moving South for decades, drawn by lower costs, weak unions and local government incentives. Foreign automakers have often chosen sites in the South when they set up factories in the United States. \uc2 BMW\uc2  and \uc2 Volvo Cars\uc2  have factories in South Carolina; Mercedes and Hyundai in Alabama; \uc2 Toyota\uc2  in Kentucky; and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Most of the foreign automakers do not disclose what they pay their workers. \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , an exception, said the starting pay at its factory in Chattanooga, Tenn., was $21.10 for hourly production workers. Veteran workers earn more than $29, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Foreign automakers concentrated in the South sometimes pay their U.S. workers more than Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , according to a study by EY for Autos Drive America, an industry association that represents \uc2 Nissan\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , Mercedes and others.\par \par \uc2 The average starting wage at foreign automakers was $19 an hour, the survey said, more than the $17 starting wage for U.A.W. members. But the average maximum pay at the foreign automakers was $28, compared with $32 for U.A.W. members under the current contract.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Nissan\uc2  spokesman declined to say how much the company pays its U.S. workers, but he said the average was higher than that reported by the Autos Drive America survey.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which is based in Texas and has factories there and in Buffalo; Fremont, Calif.; and Sparks, Nev., does not disclose what it pays its workers, but the Detroit automakers say it is less than what they pay.\par \par \uc2 Ford has said its labor costs, including benefits and bonuses, are 40 percent more per worker than \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's. That figure does not include stock awards that at least some \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees receive. On \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's website, job advertisements for a production associate pay $20 to $23 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Even if autoworker pay in Alabama or Mississippi is less than what it is in Michigan or Illinois, it is often more than what employers in other industries pay in those places.\par \par \uc2 Working conditions are often a bigger issue than pay, labor representatives say.\par \par \uc2 In February, Emily Erickson of the \uc2 University of Warwick\uc2  in England and Berneece Herbert of \uc2 Jackson State University\uc2  published a survey of 211 workers at Mercedes's factory in Vance, Ala., near Tuscaloosa.\par \par \uc2 The workers reported earning an average of $27 an hour at Mercedes, high for the region. But they said they were forced to work overtime or change their work schedules with little notice. Almost half worked more than 50 hours a week. The study also found that white workers made an average of $3 more per hour than Black workers.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes denied that it discriminates. ''Our pay structure is equal for all team members regardless of race, age or ethnic origin, and our pay progressions are based on seniority,'' the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 It noted that the company employed 6,000 people in Alabama, suggesting that the study sampled too few workers. ''We do not agree with its conclusions,'' Mercedes said.\par \par \uc2 The gulf between pay in the South and the North is certain to widen when Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agree on new contracts with the U.A.W. The union is demanding a 40 percent increase over four years. Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have already offered raises of 23 percent and could go higher.\par \par \uc2 Unions have made some progress in the South recently. Workers at \uc2 Blue Bird\uc2 , which makes school buses in Georgia, voted to join the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  in May and are negotiating a contract. Workers at ZF, which makes axles for Mercedes in Alabama, ended a monthlong strike last week after the German company agreed to raise the top hourly wage to $23.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders say they have already been deluged with calls from workers at \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and Hyundai who expressed interest in organizing unions. Workers at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  voted not to join the union in 2019, but the environment may be different this time.\par \par \uc2 ''These workers will say, 'Look what the U.A.W. did for these workers at G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ,''' said Tim Smith, director of U.A.W. Region 8, which includes all of the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 ''We've got organizers on the ground there right now,'' he said. ''We're starting to make our move.''\par \par \uc2 Ben Casselman and Bob Chiarito contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ben Casselman and Bob Chiarito contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Schataan Lyke, left, a Ford worker in Chicago, said she was glad to have the union supporting her. Morris Mock helped lead a failed union drive at Nissan. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MICHELLE LITVIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; ROBERT WAYNE LEWIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231025ejap0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
