\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press and Reuters \par \uc2 485 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AEROSPACE\par \par \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2  Near\par \par \uc2 A New Labor Contract\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  and \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2  have reached a tentative agreement over a new labor contract covering 1,100 workers at some of the U.S. defense contractor's facilities, staving off a potential strike.\par \par \uc2 The four-year deal, which must be ratified by the workers, provides a 14 percent wage increase, protects against inflation, reduces the time it takes to get to top pay and ''beats back the company's proposed health care concessions,'' the U.A.W. said.\par \par \uc2 U.A.W. members had overwhelmingly voted this month to authorize a strike after a four-year agreement was set to expire this past Sunday.\par \par \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2 , one of the largest U.S. defense contractors, has struggled with supply and labor shortages at a time when weapons demand is on the rise because of the war in Ukraine, conflict in the Middle East and tensions in U.S.-China relations over Taiwan. REUTERS\par \par \uc2 Aviation\par \par \uc2 Chief of \uc2 Qatar Airways\par \par \uc2 To Retire After 27 Years\par \par \uc2 Qatar Airways Group\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Akbar Al Baker\uc2 , right, is stepping down after 27 years leading the company.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Al Baker's retirement will go into effect Nov. 5, \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 He will be succeeded by Badr Mohammed Al-Meer -- the current chief operating officer of \uc2 Hamad International Airport\uc2  in Doha, the hub of Qatar's national carrier.\par \par \uc2 During his tenure at \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2 , Mr. Al Baker became known for being one of the most outspoken leaders in the airline industry.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Al Baker has also made some controversial comments over his career -- including suggesting that women can't run airlines and for calling American carriers ''crap'' and that their passengers were ''always being served by grandmothers.'' He later apologized for both remarks.\par \par \uc2 ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 Automobiles\par \par \uc2 Daechang Will Open\par \par \uc2 A Factory in Georgia\par \par \uc2 A South Korean company that makes automotive seat frames will open a factory in Georgia to supply a new \uc2 Hyundai Motor Group\uc2  plant.\par \par \uc2 Daechang Seat Corp., based in Gyeongju, South Korea, said on Monday that it would invest $72.5 million in a plant in Savannah, hiring more than 500 people.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest company to announce a plant to supply Hyundai's $7.6 billion plant to assemble electric vehicles and batteries in Ellabell, near Savannah. The Hyundai plant, announced last year, could grow to 8,500 employees and could begin producing vehicles as early as 2024.\par \par \uc2 Daechang Seat opened a factory in Phenix City, Ala., in 2019. The company announced a $23.4 million expansion in Phenix City last year, saying it would build a new building and hire an additional 300 people. The company employs more than 5,000 people in seven countries.\par \par \uc2 ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY GIUSEPPE CACACE/AFP -- GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231024ejao0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
