\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Ford Chairman Says Strike Is Helping \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Toyota\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 727 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mr. Ford, the executive chairman of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , said nonunion automakers would make gains against Michigan automakers because of strikes by the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The monthlong strike by the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  and the union's demands for substantial pay and benefits increases risk damaging the U.S. auto industry, hurting its ability to compete against nonunion foreign rivals, the executive chairman of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The fight should not be seen as the U.A.W. against Ford, or its crosstown rivals, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , said \uc2 William C. Ford Jr\uc2 ., the great-grandson of the company's founder \uc2 Henry Ford\uc2 , noting that at times U.A.W. officials have referred to the automakers as the union's ''enemy.''\par \par \uc2 ''It should be Ford and the U.A.W. against \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and all the Chinese companies that want to enter our home market,'' Mr. Ford said at the company's Rouge plant in Dearborn, Mich.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and the others are loving the strike, because they know the longer it goes on, the better it is for them,'' the executive chairman said. ''They will win, and all of us will lose.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ford's remarks alluded to a period several decades ago when the U.A.W. won increasingly rich contracts that were later seen by many industry experts as having hobbled the three Michigan automakers in the face of competition from Japanese and European carmakers. Ford came to the brink of collapse, and G.M. and Chrysler -- now part of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- had to seek bankruptcy protection after the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 ''Ford's ability to invest in the future isn't just a talking point,'' Mr. Ford said. ''It is the absolute lifeblood of our company. And if we lose it, we will lose to the competition. Many jobs will be lost.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the U.A.W. president, Shawn Fain, said Mr. Ford should ''stop playing games'' and meet the union's demands, or ''we'll close the Rouge for him.'' Mr. Fain added that the U.A.W. was not fighting the company but ''corporate greed.''\par \par \uc2 ''If Ford wants to be the all-American auto company, they can pay all-American wages and benefits,'' Mr. Fain said. ''Workers at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2  and others are not the enemy -- they're the U.A.W. members of the future.''\par \par \uc2 Ford, G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have been negotiating new labor contracts with the U.A.W. since July. Over the past month, the union has called on workers at a few plants to go on strike. The action has idled three Ford plants, two G.M. factories and one \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant. Workers at 38 G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  spare-parts warehouses are also on strike.\par \par \uc2 The strategy is intended to increase pressure on the companies to meet the union's demands for significantly higher wages, shorter working hours and expanded pensions, and to end a system that pays new hires just over half of the top U.A.W. wage of $32 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The companies have offered wage increases of more than 20 percent over the next four years and certain other measures in line with the union's demands, but the U.A.W. is pressing for greater concessions.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the union called for a strike by 8,700 workers at Ford's Kentucky truck plant in Louisville, the company's largest.\par \par \uc2 Ford executives said last week that the company had made a record offer to the union and that sweetening the deal would hurt the automaker's ability to invest in electric vehicles and other new models and technologies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ford, who has had a role in every round of negotiations with the U.A.W. since 1982, said the talks had reached ''a crossroads'' and warned that labor contracts that burdened the automakers with heavy costs could affect the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 ''The price of failure should be clear to everyone,'' he said. ''Let's come together and reach an agreement, so we can take the fight to the real competition.''\par \par \uc2 William C. Ford Jr. has negotiated with the U.A.W. since 1982. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CARLOS OSORIO/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231017ejah00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
