\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Federal Firefighters Face Steep Pay Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Madeleine Ngo \par \uc2 1,074 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A temporary pay raise for federal wild-land firefighters is set to end next month. Officials have said that more workers will quit if lawmakers fail to pass a permanent increase.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of federal firefighters could face substantial pay cuts in the coming weeks, potentially hampering the country's ability to respond to wildfires as they have grown more severe.\par \par \uc2 Federal wild-land firefighters received a temporary boost to their paychecks last year, which was meant to help strained agencies offer more competitive wages to recruit and retain workers. But funding for the pay raises is set to run dry next month, and federal officials have warned that more firefighters will leave for higher-paying jobs if their salaries are slashed.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, President Biden raised the minimum wage for wild-land firefighters to $15 an hour from $13. Congress then agreed to increase firefighter pay by either 50 percent of a worker's base salary or $20,000 a year, whichever was lower.\par \par \uc2 The typical base salary for an entry-level wild-land firefighter is about $34,000 without the supplement, according to Agriculture Department data. Many also rely on overtime pay to boost their incomes.\par \par \uc2 The temporary supplement was intended to serve as a ''bridge for two years as the administration works with Congress on longer-term reforms,'' according to a White House statement. While there is some bipartisan support for a permanent pay raise, it is unclear whether Congress will approve an increase.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are pushing for deep spending cuts, and disagreements over funding levels nearly led to a government shutdown late last month. The removal of Representative Kevin McCarthy as House speaker has also thrown that chamber into disarray, raising the chances of a shutdown next month.\par \par \uc2 The pay cuts are looming at a moment when climate change has intensified the risk of wildfires, which have grown larger, spread faster and become more destructive in recent years. Warmer temperatures and drier conditions have also led to longer fire seasons, increasing the burden on federal firefighters who respond to both fires on federal land and assist state and local fire departments.\par \par \uc2 The federal government has long struggled to hire firefighters, largely because wages have lagged behind some state and local counterparts. Many workers could also earn more money at less strenuous jobs.\par \par \uc2 The National Federation of Federal Employees, which represents about 10,000 wild-land firefighters, has estimated that 30 to 50 percent of the work force could resign if salary increases end.\par \par \uc2 Mike Alba, 43, an assistant engine captain residing in Santa Clarita, Calif., said he would have to go back to ''living paycheck to paycheck'' if his annual base pay dropped to about $52,000 from $72,000. He said the pay increase has allowed him to spend more time with his two children because he has felt less pressure to work extra hours, but he would probably quit if his salary was reduced.\par \par \uc2 ''I would have to work twice as hard just to make ends meet,'' said Mr. Alba, who has worked for the Forest Service for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Alba said his station was already struggling to fill three open positions and two firefighters recently left to take jobs at state and municipal fire departments.\par \par \uc2 ''If we can't staff, that means we can't go to fires,'' Mr. Alba said. ''That means loss of property. It could mean loss of life.''\par \par \uc2 A temporary spending measure to keep the government funded allows federal officials to tap into other funding sources to continue paying the salary supplement through mid-November, but officials said that fell short of a permanent solution.\par \par \uc2 ''Without a permanent pay fix that creates certainty for our federal firefighters at both the Forest Service and the Department of the Interior, we will continue to see wild-land firefighters leave their jobs for other, higher-paying jobs,'' a representative for the Agriculture Department, which oversees the \uc2 U.S. Forest Service\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The Forest Service, which employs roughly 11,000 seasonal and permanent wild-land firefighters, previously estimated that funding would run out around the beginning of November. Officials at the Interior Department, which employs about 5,000 wild-land fire personnel, also said the funding was set to run out around then.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Joe Neguse\uc2 , a Colorado Democrat and co-chairman of the Bipartisan Wildfire Caucus, has introduced bipartisan legislation that would permanently increase federal firefighter pay, but the measure has so far stalled in the House.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm deeply concerned about the inability of Congress to muster the political will to get this done,'' Mr. Neguse said. ''The reality is that our wild-land firefighters are woefully undercompensated.''\par \par \uc2 Randy Erwin, the president of the National Federation of Federal Employees, said he felt ''reasonably confident'' that pay increases would be made permanent, but the Republican push for spending cuts made that outcome more uncertain and many firefighters were already departing or crafting contingency plans.\par \par \uc2 ''They're going to be leaving in droves,'' Mr. Erwin said. ''It's a really scary thing to think that we won't have the necessary personnel to contain wildfire in this country.''\par \par \uc2 Chris Hensley, 36, a federal firefighter in southwest Colorado, said he was able to save up to buy a house last month because his annual base pay rose to $60,000. He said he worried about affording his mortgage payments if his base pay dropped to $40,000, but he planned to stay at his position for now.\par \par \uc2 ''Taking care of the forest health is something that we all take a lot of pride in, so it keeps us here,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Hensley said he would consider leaving because he could easily earn more or a similar amount elsewhere. ''If they can't figure it out and keep paying us what they're paying us now,'' he said, ''I could go work at the local pizza shop.''\par \par \uc2 The union representing about 10,000 federal wildland firefighters estimates 30 to 50 percent of the work force would quit. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JUNGHO KIM FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Mike Alba's base pay would drop to $52,000 from $72,000, and ''I would have to work twice as hard just to make ends meet.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY PHILIP CHEUNG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A23. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231015ejaf0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
