\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.A.W.'s Leader Says Strikes Will Become More Unpredictable\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 716 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  refrained from expanding the strikes at Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  but said it could do so at any time.\par \par \uc2 Four weeks after starting limited strikes against three large automakers, the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  is shifting to a more aggressive strategy, suggesting that work stoppages could spread to more plants and possibly go on for some time.\par \par \uc2 In an online video, the union's president, Shawn Fain, said on Friday that he would no longer wait to announce expansions of the strikes on Friday, as he had been doing. Further actions could come at any time.\par \par \uc2 ''We're not messing around,'' Mr. Fain said. ''The companies are now on notice. If they're not willing to move, we are going to give them a push.''\par \par \uc2 The union began its strikes on Sept. 15 when workers walked out of three plants, owned by \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which makes Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram vehicles. It has since expanded the strike in stages, in a bid to increase the pressure on the companies.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said on Friday that it was temporarily laying off an additional 700 workers at two plants in Indiana that supply transmissions and castings for a Toledo, Ohio, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  factory that has been idled by the U.A.W. strikes. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has laid off more than 1,300 workers in total in response to the union's strikes.\par \par \uc2 Ford has laid off more than 1,900 workers as a result of the strike, and G.M. about 2,300. Ford said about 90 of its parts suppliers had laid off about 13,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  also said its negotiations had made progress in talks with the U.A.W. this week. The company said it hoped ''to reach an agreement as soon as possible to get everyone back to work.''\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. and the automakers have been negotiating new labor contracts since July.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the U.A.W. unexpectedly told workers to walk out of Ford's Kentucky Truck Plant in Louisville. It is the company's largest and the producer of its highly profitable Super Duty version of its F-Series pickup trucks.\par \par \uc2 Ford has said the Kentucky plant typically produces a new truck every 37 seconds, and generates $25 billion in revenue, about 16 percent of the company's annual total.\par \par \uc2 All told, the strike has halted operations at three Ford plants in Michigan, Chicago and Kentucky; two G.M. plants in Michigan and Missouri; and a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant in Ohio. U.A.W. members are also on strike at 38 G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  spare-parts warehouses across the country.\par \par \uc2 About 34,000 of the 150,000 U.A.W. members employed by the three companies are now on strike.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. has demanded substantial wage increases and improvements in other areas of its contract, like retirement plans. The union also wants an end to a system that pays new hires a little over half the top U.A.W. wage of $32 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The union is also concerned about the possible loss of jobs as automakers ramp up production of electric vehicles. The companies have offered wage increases of more than 20 percent over four years and to reduce the time it takes a new worker to rise to the top wage to four years from eight.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Ford officials said the company had reached its limit on what it could offer the union without hurting the company's business and its ability to continue heavy investments in electric vehicles. ''Any more will stretch our ability to invest in the business,'' \uc2 Kumar Galhotra\uc2 , president of the Ford division that makes combustion engine vehicles, said on a conference call on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Apart from the car companies, U.A.W.-represented workers went on strike this week at \uc2 Mack Trucks\uc2 . Its members voted this week to authorize a strike against \uc2 General Dynamics\uc2 , an aerospace and defense contractor. The U.A.W. also represents about 1,000 workers who have been on strike at \uc2 Blue Cross Blue Shield of Michigan\uc2  for a month.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231014ejae0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
