\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Treads Water Ahead of Report on Job Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street drifted to a quiet close Thursday as pressure from the bond market remained high because of worries about a too-hot U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 slipped 0.1 percent a day before a highly anticipated report on the job market that could sway the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's view on interest rates. The Dow Jones industrial average edged down by less than 0.1 percent, and the Nasdaq composite dipped 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have struggled since the summer under the weight of soaring Treasury yields in the bond market. Traders have acquiesced to a new normal where the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is likely to keep its main interest rate at a high level for a long time as it tries to bring inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields wavered up and down Thursday after a report showed fewer U.S. workers applied for unemployment benefits last week than economists expected. That's a sign fewer workers are getting laid off than expected.\par \par \uc2 The worry is that a strong job market could put upward pressure on inflation. The Fed has raised its main interest rate to the highest level since 2001.\par \par \uc2 ''Even as the Fed has taken aggressive action to soften labor market conditions, businesses continue to hold on to workers,'' said Rubeela Farooqi, chief U.S. economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A more comprehensive report on the overall U.S. job market is due Friday, and economists expect it to show hiring slowed to a pace of 163,000 jobs added in September from 187,000 in August.\par \par \uc2 After initially jumping on the jobless claims report, the yield on the 10-year Treasury later pulled back. The 10-year yield was at 4.71 percent, down from 4.73 percent late Wednesday. Earlier this week, it hit its highest level since 2007.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year Treasury is the centerpiece of the bond market, and movements in its yield ripple across the entire economy. Where it's heading in the next three to six months has a wide range of potential outcomes, according to Bruno Braizinha, rates strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Braizinha said the yield could drop back to 4 percent to 4.25 percent if upcoming reports show the economy weakening enough to undercut inflation but not so much that it causes a sharp recession.\par \par \uc2 But he also said in a BofA Global Research report that it could top 5.50 percent if economic data strengthen so much it pushes the Fed to keep raising rates.\par \par \uc2 A recent pullback in the price of oil has offered some relief on the inflation front for both U.S. households and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After charging from $70 in the summer to more than $93 last week, the price of a barrel of benchmark U.S. crude has slumped sharply. It fell another $1.91 to settle at $82.31, a day after tumbling more than $5 for its worst drop in more than a year. Brent crude, the international standard, lost $1.74 to $84.07 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Clorox\uc2  fell 5.2 percent after the company described how big a loss it expects to take for its latest quarter because of a previously disclosed cybersecurity attack. The company said its shipments had been in line with its expectations before the attack caused widespread disruptions.\par \par \uc2 Rivian Automotive\uc2  sank 22.9 percent after the electric vehicle maker said it will raise $1.5 billion by selling debt that could later convert into stock.\par \par \uc2 On the winning side was \uc2 Lamb Weston\uc2 , which sells frozen fries, hash browns and other potato products. It jumped 8 percent after reporting stronger profit for its latest quarter than analysts expected. The company also raised its profit forecast for the fiscal year, saying it's benefiting after raising prices for its products.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, indexes moved only modestly across much of Europe and of Asia. Japan's Nikkei 225 was an outlier and jumped 1.8 percent. It's been one of the world's better stock markets this year.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231006eja600055\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
