\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The U.A.W. Escalates Its Strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 1,571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  said 7,000 more of its members would walk off the job two weeks after it began strikes at the Big Three automakers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  increased the pressure on \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  by extending its strike to two more car assembly plants on Friday, saying the companies had not moved far enough to meet its demands for higher pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 The move is the second escalation of strikes that started on Sept. 15 at three plants, one each owned by G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent of Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram. The union said it would not expand the strike against \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  this week because of progress in negotiations there.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W.'s president, Shawn Fain, said workers at a Ford plant in Chicago and a G.M. factory in Lansing, Mich., would walk off the job on Friday. G.M. makes the Buick Enclave and Chevrolet Traverse sport-utility vehicles at the Lansing plant. Ford makes the Explorer, the Police Interceptor Utility and Lincoln Aviator in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 ''Ford and G.M. have refused to make meaningful progress at the bargaining table,'' Mr. Fain said in a live-streamed video.\par \par \uc2 Ford's Chicago plant employs about 4,600 U.A.W. members and G.M.'s Lansing plant has 2,300 union workers. Including the workers who walked off the job earlier, more than 25,000 U.A.W. members at the three companies have been called on to stop working. The three automakers together employ nearly 150,000 U.A.W. members.\par \par \uc2 A week ago, workers walked out at 38 spare-parts distribution centers owned by G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The U.A.W. did not expand its strike at Ford because, the union said at the time, it had made significant progress in contract negotiations with that company.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. is seeking a substantial wage increase for workers and opened the talks by demanding a 40 percent raise, pointing to the substantial profits all three companies have generated over the last decade and to the size of the pay increases for their chief executives over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 The companies have each offered roughly 20 percent over four years. Ford and the union have reached agreements on some other demands, including cost-of-living adjustments if inflation surges again, and the right to strike if the company closes plants.\par \par \uc2 ''Fain is out-negotiating the car companies, and he is having fun making them dance while he calls them names,'' said \uc2 Erik Gordon\uc2 , a business professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  who follows the auto industry. ''One week he gets Ford to give more in the hope of not being targeted for another closure. The next week he tells Ford they haven't given enough and closes one of their plants.''\par \par \uc2 But if the companies agree to most of the union's demands, they could struggle to compete in the fast-growing market for electric vehicles, which is dominated by \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , a nonunion automaker, Professor Gordon said. ''The union will enjoy big gains for a few years until the companies' inability to compete causes job losses,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The parties have met regularly, and on Thursday the union presented its latest counteroffer to \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the union said. Negotiating teams from the U.A.W. and G.M. met on Wednesday in a session attended by Mr. Fain.\par \par \uc2 The union leader's online remarks on Friday were delayed for nearly half an hour by what he called ''a flurry of interest from the companies in addressing some serious bargaining issue.'' He did not provide details.\par \par \uc2 Ford's chief executive, \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , said on Friday that the company and the U.A.W. were ''very close'' to a deal but remained apart on potential contract terms for workers at four electric vehicle battery factories the company is building. ''If the U.A.W.'s goal is a record contract, they already have that,'' he told reporters on a conference call.\par \par \uc2 In the company's view, discussions about the battery plants should not hold up the negotiations on a new four-year contract because they won't be completed for two years or more.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. sees things differently. Union leaders are concerned that automakers will use the transition to electric vehicles to lower wages and reduce the number of unionized workers they employ.\par \par \uc2 The union wants to include the workers at battery factories owned partly or fully by automakers in their national contracts with the U.A.W. Mr. Fain has said the workers at battery factories are exposed to more dangerous working conditions yet are paid much less than union members at vehicle assembly plants.\par \par \uc2 The automakers have said that they cannot include battery factory workers in their national contracts because most of the plants are set up as joint ventures with foreign companies like \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2  and SK On.\par \par \uc2 Among the three automakers, only G.M. has started producing batteries, at a plant it jointly owns with \uc2 LG Energy Solution\uc2  in \uc2 Lordstown\uc2 , Ohio. Ford is building three battery plants in Kentucky and Tennessee with SK On.\par \par \uc2 Ford said this week that it would halt work on another battery plant, wholly owned by the automaker, that it had planned to build in Marshall, Mich. because it was not certain that it could make products there at a competitive price. ''We will decide how big or small Marshall will be,'' Mr. Farley said, once Ford has a better idea of how much it will cost to make batteries there.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Farley said the start of production at battery plants would not result in the loss of U.A.W. jobs elsewhere at Ford. The company employs 57,000 U.A.W. members, more than at G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Mr. Fain disputed Ford's characterization of the talks. He said that the U.A.W. was waiting for a response from the company to a ''comprehensive proposal'' the union made on Monday. Mr. Fain said the two sides were still ''far apart'' on retirement benefits and workers' job security in the transition to electric vehicles. ''Name the time and the place you want to settle a fair contract for our members, and we'll be there,'' Mr. Fain said.\par \par \uc2 G.M.'s chief executive, \uc2 Mary T. Barra\uc2 , criticized the union for ''upping the rhetoric and the theatrics'' and said that the U.A.W.'s leaders had ''no real intent to get to an agreement.''\par \par \uc2 ''We need the U.A.W. leadership at the bargaining table with the clear intent of reaching an agreement now,'' she said in a statement. ''For them to do otherwise is putting our collective future at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said that it had made progress in the talks but that ''gaps remain.'' The company said it ''has been intensely working with the U.A.W. to find solutions to the issues that are of most concern to our employees while ensuring the company can remain competitive.''\par \par \uc2 Tensions on the picket lines have flared this week. The union said five strikers on the picket line suffered minor injuries when they were hit by a car outside a G.M. plant in Flint, Mich. Other confrontations occurred at picket lines in California, Massachusetts and Michigan, the union said.\par \par \uc2 ''We will not be intimidated into backing down,'' said Mr. Fain, who has frequently compared the strike to a ''war on corporate greed.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Thursday, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  criticized Mr. Fain's characterization of the negotiations, and blamed the union for violence, saying that some strikers had slashed tires on trucks and harassed nonstriking employees at parts warehouses.\par \par \uc2 ''The deliberate use of inflammatory and violent rhetoric is dangerous and needs to stop,'' \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said. ''The companies are not 'the enemy' and we are not at 'war.' We respect our employees' right to advocate for their position, including their right to peacefully picket. But the violence must stop.''\par \par \uc2 The strategy of striking at only a limited number of locations, but spreading the walkouts to plants owned by all three automakers, is a break from U.A.W.'s traditional approach of idling most or all operations at one company. In 2019, union workers went on strike at G.M. for 40 days before a tentative agreement was reached.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fain has said the strategy is intended to keep the companies guessing about what parts of their operations would be hit next, in hopes of improving the union's negotiating position. The first three plants hit by the strike make some of the automakers' most profitable vehicles, including the Chevrolet Colorado, Ford Bronco and Jeep Wrangler.\par \par \uc2 A limited strike also dents the companies' profits while limiting damage to their suppliers, local businesses and the national economy.\par \par \uc2 Expanding the strike also increases the financial cost to the union. It is paying striking workers $500 a week out of its $825 million strike fund.\par \par \uc2 Santul Nerkar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Santul Nerkar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Picketing outside Ford's Chicago assembly plant on Friday. In total, more than 25,000 U.A.W. members have been called on to stop working. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TAYLOR GLASCOCK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B4) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230930ej9u0004d\par }\page {