\par\par \uc2 TIMES INSIDER\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Strikes and Deep Roots: 'Good Stories in Detroit'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emmett Lindner \par \uc2 973 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As electric vehicles usher in a new era for the car economy and workers strike against rooted manufacturers, Neal E. Boudette is in Motor City to cover it all.\par \par \uc2 Times Insider explains who we are and what we do and delivers behind-the-scenes insights into how our journalism comes together.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in the nearly 90-year history of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , the union is on strike against all three of the large Detroit automakers -- \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent company of Chrysler -- at the same time.\par \par \uc2 The targeted strike, which started on Sept. 15 and expanded last Friday, has shut down three auto plants in Michigan, Ohio and Missouri, and 38 parts distribution centers operated by G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . About 18,300 U.A.W. members are on strike while the union demands a 40 percent pay increase. The shutdowns affect only a few car models, but a protracted strike would affect consumers and the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Neal E. Boudette, who covers the auto industry from Detroit for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , has been reporting on the negotiations between the union and automakers. He has covered the automobile industry since 2002, when he was in Germany working for The Wall Street Journal. He joined The Times in 2016.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Mr. Boudette spoke about the evolution of the industry, its symbolism in the United States and what this strike could mean for other automakers. This interview has been edited and condensed.\par \par \uc2 President Biden visited a picket line in Michigan this week, which has been described as unprecedented for a sitting president. Does this strike feel different from U.A.W.'s strike at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in 2019, or ones before that?\par \par \uc2 A lot of the strikes in the past 20 years have been symbolic more than strategic. This year, it's a totally different dynamic. The union has a very strong negotiating position. The car companies are making tons of money, and workers have not received raises that have kept up with inflation. The workers have a lot of support from the public.\par \par \uc2 There have been strikes in the past that were very contentious and even resulted in street clashes. But in the 21st century, there hasn't been a strike like this.\par \par \uc2 You worked in Germany before coming to Detroit. How do the settings compare?\par \par \uc2 Detroit is Motor City. The whole region has this very strong connection to automobiles. There's a century-long history and a great deal of culture built around the automobile. The \uc2 Detroit Red Wings\uc2  -- their logo is a winged wheel.\par \par \uc2 It was less so in Germany. I was based in Frankfurt, not an auto center. \uc2 Opel\uc2  was a little bit outside of Frankfurt, and Mercedes was south of Frankfurt. B.M.W. was down in Munich; \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  was up north in Wolfsburg. Each of those towns is a little like Detroit, but you didn't have the concentration that you have in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 How has the evolution of electric vehicles changed your beat?\par \par \uc2 The biggest trend is electrification, and there are many different aspects of it. Aside from the fact that you have to get these cars designed and developed and produced, there's a social aspect to it. We need chargers all over the place, and charging a car is a different social experience than filling up your gas tank.\par \par \uc2 Then there's the climate aspect. The evolution of the industry to electronic vehicles has been called the biggest transition the industry has seen since the introduction of the moving assembly line.\par \par \uc2 The beat is multifaceted now. Before the financial crisis, it wasn't quite like that. There was a technology race, but it was a very limited technology race. And now it's taking on a much greater breadth.\par \par \uc2 What affects will the strike have on the industry?\par \par \uc2 Eventually the union and automakers will come up with an agreement. And then the next big question will be: How much of a burden does this contract put on these companies? Their competitors -- \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , \uc2 Kia\uc2  -- are foreign companies who operate nonunion factories. Most of their workers make considerably less than the top U.A.W. wage.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, U.A.W. tried to organize a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Tennessee and a \uc2 Nissan\uc2  plant in Mississippi, and the workers voted down union representation. They didn't want to rock the boat.\par \par \uc2 But there's a lot of power on the side of workers these days. I think it's possible workers in the South say: We're making $22 an hour. The workers in Detroit are making $32 an hour. We should be in the union.\par \par \uc2 As an auto industry reporter, are you a big car guy?\par \par \uc2 It's funny, I'm not. I'm interested in cars, but I'm not a big car person. I drive a Chevy Malibu, believe it or not.\par \par \uc2 Cars are interesting products to cover. There's no other country in the world where there are as many pop songs and movies about cars. The Beach Boys' ''409''; that David Hasselhoff TV show, ''Knight Rider.'' It's a whole TV show built around a car.\par \par \uc2 There are good stories in Detroit. Sometimes it's a matter of explaining things to readers. We did a lot of that in the past year on the transition to E.V.s. There are also really interesting personalities to write about. That's what keeps me going.\par \par \uc2 The United Auto Workers held a practice picket line outside of the headquarters of the automaker Stellantis in Auburn Hills, Mich., on Sept. 20. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK HAGEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230929ej9t00052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
